{"title":"Comparing and testing outcomes","markdown":{"headingText":"Comparing and testing outcomes","containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\nFrom the boxplot, it looked like the intervention arm had more remembered digits than the control arm. Now we summarize these values, and test for differences between the two groups.\n\n```{r}\n#| message: false\nlibrary(tidyverse)\ntheme_set(theme_bw(base_size = 12))\nlibrary(gtsummary)\n```\n\n```{r}\n#| code-fold: true\nset.seed(20261019)\nn <- 60\ndat <- tibble(study_id = 1:(2 * n),\n              first = sample(LETTERS, 2 * n, replace = TRUE),\n              last = sample(LETTERS, 2 * n, replace = TRUE),\n              inclusion_over17 = 1,\n              inclusion2 = 1, inclusion3 = 1, inclusion4 = 1,\n              exclusion1 = 0, exclusion2 = 0,\n              screened = 1,\n              informed_consent = rbinom(2 * n, 1, 0.9),\n              endpoints =  rbinom(2 * n, 1, 0.9),\n              age_today = floor(runif(2 * n, 25, 45)),\n              gender = rbinom(2 * n, 2, 0.250),\n              video_games = rbinom(2 * n, 4, 0.2),\n              randomization_group = rep(0:1, n)) |> \n  mutate(reverse_digit = rnorm(2 * n, 3 + 2 * randomization_group, 1.5),\n         reverse_digit = round(reverse_digit),\n         cold_pressor = rnorm(2 * n, 60 - 10 * randomization_group, 20),\n         cold_pressor = round(cold_pressor),\n         video_challenge = rbinom(2 * n, 1, 0.2 + 0.3 * randomization_group)) |> \n  mutate(across(age_today:video_challenge, ~ ifelse(informed_consent == 1, .x, NA))) |> \n  mutate(across(reverse_digit:video_challenge, ~ ifelse(endpoints == 1, .x, NA))) |> \n  select(-endpoints) \n\nsm <- dat |> \n  select(-(inclusion_over17:screened)) |> \n  rename(id = study_id,\n         trt = randomization_group,\n         age = age_today) |> \n  mutate(gender = factor(gender, 0:2, c(\"F\", \"M\", \"O\")),\n         trt = factor(trt, 0:1, c(\"control\", \"intervention\")),\n         video_games = factor(video_games, 0:3, \n                              c(\"<30 minutes per day\", \"30-60\", \"60-120\", \"120+\"))) |> \n  filter(!is.na(trt), !is.na(reverse_digit))\n```\n\nJust like we did earlier with the demographics table, let's make a summary table using `tbl_summary()`.\n\n```{r primarytable}\nsm |> \n  select(trt, reverse_digit, cold_pressor, video_challenge) |> \n  tbl_summary(by = trt, \n              type = list(reverse_digit ~ \"continuous\"), \n              statistic = list(reverse_digit ~ \"{mean} ({sd})\",\n                               cold_pressor ~ \"{mean} ({sd})\"),\n              label = list(reverse_digit ~ \"reverse digit span test\",\n                           cold_pressor ~ \"cold pressor test\",\n                           video_challenge ~ \"video challenge\"))\n```\n\nThe difference between these two means is exactly what we should get from a two-sample t-test, or linear regression. If we use linear regression, it's quite easy to get publication-ready output using `tbl_regression()`from the `gtsummary` package.\n\n```{r linearmod-example, echo = TRUE}\nmod <- lm(reverse_digit ~ trt, data = sm)\ntbl_regression(mod, conf.int = TRUE)\n```\n\nNow try to run a similar model for `cold_pressor`.\n\n```{r linear}\nmod <- lm(cold_pressor ~ trt, data = sm)\ntbl_regression(mod, conf.int = TRUE)\n```\n\nAnd for binary outcomes like the `video_challenge`, we can run logistic regression. While there is no function in R to run a logistic regression model directly, we can use `glm()` with the option `family = binomial`. To get odds ratios, we put the option `exponentiate = TRUE` in `tbl_regression()`.\n\n```{r logitmod-example}\nmod <- glm(video_challenge ~ trt, data = sm,\n           family = binomial)\ntbl_regression(mod, conf.int = TRUE, \n               exponentiate = TRUE)\n```\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina